ravel
=====

A toy JavaScript/TypeScript runtime written in Rust, built on QuickJS and
Oxc. It exists to learn how runtimes like Node, Deno, and Bun work, not to
be used for anything real.

Licensed under the MIT License. See LICENSE.


Usage
-----

	ravel file.js           run JavaScript
	ravel file.ts           run TypeScript (auto-transpiled via Oxc)
	ravel                   start the REPL
	ravel --build file.js   SSG build mode (one-off, no timers)
	ravel --serve           serve dist/ on port 3000
	ravel --serve 8080      serve dist/ on a custom port
	ravel --help
	ravel --version


Building
--------

	cargo build             debug build at target/debug/ravel
	cargo install --path .  install `ravel` on your PATH
	cargo test              run the tests

`cargo build` only writes to target/debug/. Rerun `cargo install --path .`
after changing the source, or the `ravel` on your PATH stays at the last
installed version.


Features
--------

ES6+ via QuickJS
	Promises, async/await, classes, modules, and so on.

TypeScript
	.ts and .tsx files are stripped of types by Oxc and fed directly
	into QuickJS.

JSX rendering
	.tsx files transform JSX into note() calls that produce HTML
	strings. See "JSX" below.

Async runtime
	setTimeout, setInterval, clearTimeout, clearInterval on a Tokio
	event loop. Promise callbacks and await continuations are drained
	before exit.

Error reporting
	Uncaught errors and unhandled promise rejections print name,
	message, and stack to stderr, and exit with status 1. See "Error
	reporting" below.

Sandboxed fs
	fs.readFile, fs.writeFile (accepts a string or bytes, auto-creates
	parent dirs), fs.mkdirSync, fs.exists, all scoped to the script's
	directory. Path traversal, symlink escape, and null byte attacks
	are blocked.

Text encoding
	TextEncoder and TextDecoder. UTF-8 only; other labels throw
	RangeError rather than silently mis-decoding.

console
	log, info, and debug go to stdout; warn and error go to stderr.

ESM imports
	Relative and bare imports with .js, .mjs, .ts, .tsx resolution.

Globals
	__filename, __dirname, process.env, process.argv, process.exit,
	ravel.version, ravel.build.

REPL
	Line history with persistence, and timer support.

SSG build mode
	--build runs a script as a one-off compilation task, with
	ravel.build === true and process.env.RAVEL_BUILD === "1".

Dev server
	--serve serves the dist/ directory over HTTP (default port 3000).
	--base strips a path prefix for subpath deployments.


Project structure
-----------------

	src/
	  core.rs       module declarations for core/
	  core/         QuickJS runtime, module loader, event loop
	  transpiler.rs Oxc TypeScript-to-JavaScript transpiler (with JSX)
	  jsx.rs        note() HTML renderer (Hono-style JSX runtime)
	  console.rs    console log/info/debug/warn/error
	  encoding.rs   TextEncoder / TextDecoder
	  error.rs      error formatting + unhandled rejection tracking
	  fs.rs         sandboxed filesystem
	  timer.rs      setTimeout / setInterval
	  config.rs     ravel.json config loader
	  cli.rs        module declarations for cli/
	  cli/          CLI entry point
	examples/       usage demos
	tests/          integration + snapshot tests


Error reporting
---------------

Uncaught errors print the error name, message, and a stack trace of user
frames to stderr, and the process exits with status 1 so failures are
visible in CI:

	$ ravel err.js
	Uncaught TypeError: cannot read property 'x' of null
	    at inner (err.js:1:18)
	    at outer (err.js:2:19)
	    at <anonymous> (err.js:3:1)
	$ echo $?
	1

Promise rejections that are never handled are reported the same way:

	$ ravel rej.js
	Unhandled promise rejection: Error: nope
	    at <anonymous> (rej.js:1:19)

A rejection that gets a handler -- including one attached in a later
microtask, as await does -- is not reported. See examples/errors.js.


JSX
---

Write .tsx files with JSX syntax and they are transpiled and executed
automatically:

	// example.tsx
	const el = <div class="card"><h1>Hello</h1></div>;
	console.log(el);
	// <div class="card"><h1>Hello</h1></div>

	function Badge(props) {
	  return <span class="badge">{props.text}</span>;
	}
	console.log(<Badge text="New" />);
	// <span class="badge">New</span>

JSX is transformed using the classic runtime with note as the pragma
function. note() is injected into the QuickJS global scope and renders JSX
to HTML strings, supporting elements with attributes, nested children,
self-closing void tags (<br>, <img>), fragments (<>...</>), function
components with props, and attribute value escaping.


SSG build mode
--------------

Use --build to run a script as a one-off static site generation task.
Unlike normal execution, build mode sets ravel.build to true (vs false in
normal and REPL mode), sets process.env.RAVEL_BUILD to "1", and skips the
timer event loop.

	// build.js
	if (ravel.build) {
	  console.log("Running in build mode");
	  fs.writeFile("dist/index.html", "<h1>Built</h1>");
	}

fs.writeFile accepts a string directly. Bytes still work when you need
binary output:

	fs.writeFile("dist/index.html",
	             new TextEncoder().encode("<h1>Built</h1>"));

Build scripts can also use ESM imports, JSX components, and fs.mkdirSync
for multi-page sites:

	// components.tsx
	export function Layout(props) {
	  return (
	    <html lang="en">
	      <head><title>{props.title}</title></head>
	      <body>{props.children}</body>
	    </html>
	  );
	}

	// build.tsx
	import { Layout } from "./components.tsx";

	fs.mkdirSync("dist/blog");
	fs.writeFile(
	  "dist/index.html",
	  "<!DOCTYPE html>" + <Layout title="Home"><h1>Welcome</h1></Layout>,
	);

Then:

	ravel --build build.tsx

See examples/site/ for a complete multi-page example.


Dev server
----------

Serve the output of --build over HTTP:

	ravel --serve               serve dist/ on port 3000
	ravel --serve 8080         serve dist/ on port 8080
	ravel --base /repo --serve strip the /repo prefix

The server looks for a dist/ directory in the current working directory
and serves its contents. Unknown paths fall back to dist/index.html for
SPA-style routing.

Use --base when the site is deployed under a subpath, such as GitHub Pages
at username.github.io/repo-name/. It strips the prefix from incoming
requests so /repo-name/style.css resolves to dist/style.css.


Configuration
-------------

Create a ravel.json file in the project root to set defaults. CLI flags
override config values.

	{
	  "base": "/my-repo",
	  "port": 8080
	}

	Field  Type    Default  Description
	base   string  ""       Base path prefix to strip when serving
	port   number  3000     Port for the dev server


Filesystem
----------

fs.writeFile creates parent directories automatically, so nested static
routes need no manual directory setup:

	// creates dist/posts/hello/ along the way
	fs.writeFile("dist/posts/hello/index.html", "<h1>Hello</h1>");

fs.mkdirSync(path) creates directories recursively within the sandbox:

	fs.mkdirSync("dist/assets/css");  // creates dist/assets/css
	fs.mkdirSync("dist");             // no-op if it already exists


Build metadata
--------------

These globals are available in all modes:

	ravel.version             current ravel version, e.g. "0.3.0"
	ravel.build               true under --build, false otherwise
	process.env               all environment variables
	process.env.RAVEL_BUILD   "1" in build mode, undefined otherwise
	process.argv              [execPath, scriptPath, ...userArgs]
	process.exit(code)        exit immediately with a status code

	console.log(ravel.version);     // "0.3.0"
	console.log(process.env.HOME);  // "/Users/..."


Roadmap
-------

The goal is to learn how modern runtimes work, so the roadmap follows the
mechanisms rather than the feature checklist.

Async I/O
	Make fs return real promises backed by Tokio instead of blocking
	the thread, so a Rust future resolves a JavaScript promise. This
	is the core mechanism every runtime is built on.

A real event loop
	Replace the current 10ms polling loop with one that sleeps until
	the next timer deadline or I/O completion.

Module resolution
	Implement the node_modules lookup, package.json exports/imports
	maps, conditional exports, and self-referencing imports.

fetch and web standards
	fetch, Request/Response, URL/URLSearchParams, crypto,
	AbortController, ReadableStream/WritableStream, DOMException.

Multiple isolates
	Move the process-global timer state into the engine so more than
	one runtime can exist per process. Prerequisite for Workers.
